[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'With the release of GPT6 last week, Gaussian splatting also had quite a few major announcements conjoining the world of both generative and with reconstruction. So with that in mind, let\'s take a look at what happened this week in the world of Gaussian splatting. Radiancefields.com has tracked Gaussian'

[00:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'splatting\'s progression from the actual publication and serves the most up-to-date and accurate information. So let\'s see what happened this week. So last Monday I posted the weekly update and then Tuesday happened and we saw the release of World Lab\'s newest model'

[00:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Atlas. First model Marble had its primary output as Splat showed their newest model Atlas and it completely took over my newsfeed. Multiple of their team posts went viral and honestly I considered making a standalone video'

[00:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about it too on Tuesday. I mean, it was something that you knew was coming, but for me, I was a little bit surprised that it was here so soon. Though, in the context of everything else that happened last week, it makes a lot of sense. Their team also showed some bullet time'

[00:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reconstruction. And while it doesn\'t appear to be full six off dynamic reconstruction just yet, I did find myself revising where I thought the current state of the technology is in a very good way. And there were a ton of'

[01:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'insane examples. One of my favorite examples was this multi-room capture where they said that the entirety of it was only 30 images, which is really crazy. We\'ve ever tried to do a multi-room cap capture. 30 is not enough at all, but it looked amazing. So, I was'

[01:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely blown away by that. The other one that really blew my mind was a reconstruction of the Stanford campus from the air. And while you might be like, "Okay, that\'s cool. You just took a drone video." All of the input images for that reconstruction were from the'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ground. And the founding team of World Labs then sat down with Martin Casado from A16Z to talk a little bit more about Atlas and their approach to world models. And I actually do think it was a really great conversation. I really like this part from Ben Mildenhal. With'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Atlas, every image actually has an associated threedimensional camera pose. And that means that you can perform this task of reconstruction with an extremely high degree of precision. Right? So, if we had four views of this room, one at each corner, you can put those into the'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model and then get an exact replication of everything you see in this room, and it\'s not going to guess what\'s in the other corner, like the relationship between things. It\'s just going to reproduce exactly what you give it. We\'re saying like 50 100x reduction. And'

[02:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then that\'s at that scale where it just completely like flips that calculus on its head of like what type of captures you reconstruct. You can go back to existing imagery you have. You can go to stuff you find on the internet and even'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build scenes out of that. you can go to casual videos and like kind of unearth a lot of footage in the past we would never have treated as reconstructible uh and go back and like bring it to life as 3D potentially. This is something we\'ve been playing around with a lot with'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that. And if you don\'t know who Ben Menhal is, you really should. He is the first author of Nerf. He is an author on Dream Fusion. He\'s an author on most of the really big initial works on Nerf and uh he\'s also now a co-founder of World'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Labs. Atlas isn\'t available just yet, but they did open an early access request form on their website where you can sign up. So hopefully we\'ll be hearing back for access within the next week. I\'d imagine bunch of PRs be open for their 3JS renderer Spark. So'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hopefully we\'ll see a new release ship either this week or next week for that too. On the platform side, we saw MIMAP 3.2 release which now lets you merge both aerial and groundbased data. Nvidia released a new version of VK splatting'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which speeds up ray tracing based radiance fields like 3D Gaussian ray tracing and the 3D Gaussian unscented transform. Xgrids had a super busy week first launching LCC Studio 2.4. 4, which added both USD and ArcGIS 3D tiles'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'export. And then XGrids followed up yet again with the LCC scan app, which now supports local reconstruction from the iPhone. This has become a huge trend topic across the last couple of months'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or so, I\'d say, where there\'s been many, many apps to come out for the iPhone enabling local reconstruction. So, let me know if you\'d be interested to see some kind of breakdown as to like that capability. And um as it relates to my own app, Galaxy and Splatking, which'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also does support now local training on the device, I haven\'t made it available yet to the public, but I do have it working on my own device, too. Speaking of local iPhone reconstruction, if you\'re interested in building your own application, I would really check out'

