VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that takes 360 data and helps you reconstruct it, then put it directly into Touch Designer 2. And another processing app, 360 Splat Pro, released version 1.4.9, which I was super stoked to see because I do actually use that'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'app quite a lot for processing. basically the updates uh makes it just quite a bit more robust and solves a bunch of bugs. So, I\'m really looking forward to trying that out. Users of 3ds Max will be happy to see import and export of splats get support in the'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'TIFlow plugin. But Autodesk actually began supporting splats in the 2027.2 version, which came out, I guess, in towards the end of July. So, not super long ago. But there are a lot of ways now to begin using Gaus and splatting'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and some of these traditional software plugins. If you\'re curious to see where exactly all the total offerings are, I do maintain a total list of both the platforms and the plugins that support Gaussian spying across this page here. And at the same time too, if I\'m missing'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any of these things, please send them to me and I will make sure that they get updated accordingly. On the research side, we did get a brand new radiance field representation. Yes, Gaussian splatting is not the only type of radiance field out there. There are'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'several and this one is very interesting because instead of Gaussians, they trade it for line primitives and has a lot of cool advantages to it. Another research paper that really caught my eye was compact neural appearance models for efficient Gaussian splatting. For all of'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Gaussian splatting\'s existence, we have kind of been representing these really lifelike view dependent effects with something called spherical harmonics. And while they look amazing and very lifelike, they are very memory intensive and has drawn the question repeatedly,'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'should we be using spherical harmonics to represent these effects. So this paper examines a few alternative methods, pretty much all that have been published prior, and compares them head-to-head, but they arrive at a new neuralbased method using an MLP to actually more efficiently encode this'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'information. And what\'s even better is not only did they publish the paper, but they published an interactive viewer comparing the different options and the reconstructions. And then on top of that too, they\'ve also now published the code, which comes with an Apache 2.0 license, meaning you can use it'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'commercially. Over the past few weeks, I\'ve been speaking to the team at Bike Dance about their approach to 4D gaus and splatting and I published the findings in this article here. I would definitely recommend checking it out and'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also just generally paying attention to what some of these companies are taking a look at as it relates to dynamic. I really very strongly think Dynamic is next. Speaking of Dynamic and 4D, last week I made a joke about 4D anyone uh'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'becoming potentially available for 30 or 4090 series by a week. I was pretty much just joking. I didn\'t really think we were going to see that, but I woke up to a DM this morning from the paper\'s author with a new method called 4D any1 Turbo. And 4D anyone Turbo actually'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'maxes its aotment out at 22 GB of VRM, meaning that we can now run it on those two GPUs, which is just crazy to me. Uh, we\'ve literally gone 2 weeks from the initial publication where we needed'

[07:11] [scene:talk]
